\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Custom Literals: \texttt{lit},
\texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set},
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}}{Custom Literals: , , }}\label{ch17-custom-literals-lit-literal_set-parse_literal}

A domain-specific language frequently needs to recognise a fixed
vocabulary of named spellings: keywords such as \texttt{if},
\texttt{then}, and \texttt{else}; operators such as \texttt{+},
\texttt{-}, and \texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=}; or unit suffixes
such as \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} and \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm} that
decorate numeric literals. The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature of MuDSL
provides a small, compile-time registry for exactly these cases. A DSL
author declares a static table of literal entries, and the toolkit
generates a parsing entry point that classifies an input string against
that table at run time.

This chapter describes every component of the feature: the
\texttt{LiteralEntry} record, the
\texttt{lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} factory,
the \texttt{LiteralSet} container and its
\texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set} variadic factory, the \texttt{parse}
member that performs the lookup, the \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature tag
and its mixin, the \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} entry point on
the derived DSL, and the \texttt{HasLiterals} concept that constrains
types participating in the feature. The chapter also explains the
matching semantics in detail and illustrates the feature with worked
examples drawn from unit conversion, keyword tables, operator tables,
and a small token classifier.

The feature is intentionally narrow. It does not implement a general
lexer, nor does it compete with the parser combinators of Chapter 23 or
the PEG machinery of Chapter 27. Instead it offers a compact, typed
table that is useful as a building block inside larger front ends, and
as a convenient adapter for numeric literals that carry a unit suffix.
Readers familiar with the \texttt{FixedString} non-type template
parameter introduced in Chapter 4 will recognise the mechanism by which
literal spellings are carried at compile time.

\section{Motivation: Registering Named
Spellings}\label{ch17-motivation-registering-named-spellings}

Consider a units DSL that accepts strings such as
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km} and
\texttt{100\_\allowbreak{}cm}. The numeric part is parsed with the
standard library, while the trailing suffix selects a conversion factor.
Without a registry, the DSL author would hand-write a chain of
\texttt{if} statements comparing the tail of the string against each
known suffix. That chain is repetitive, error-prone, and detached from
the type system.

The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature replaces the hand-written chain with
a declarative table. Each row of the table is a \texttt{LiteralEntry}
that pairs a compile-time spelling with a run-time handler. The table is
aggregated into a \texttt{LiteralSet}, and the set exposes a single
\texttt{parse} function that scans its rows in order and returns the
result of the first matching handler.

The same machinery serves non-numeric vocabularies. A language with
keywords \texttt{if}, \texttt{then}, and \texttt{else} can register each
keyword as a literal whose handler yields an enumeration tag. A token
stream can then be classified by feeding each token string through the
set. The handler type is general; it is not restricted to numeric
conversion.

Because the spelling is a \texttt{FixedString} non-type template
parameter, it is fixed at compile time and the compiler can inline the
comparison. The set itself is a value of an aggregate type whose entry
list is encoded in its type, so a \texttt{LiteralSet} can be declared
\texttt{constexpr} and stored as a static member of a DSL without any
run-time initialisation cost.

\section{The LiteralEntry Record}\label{ch17-the-literalentry-record}

The unit of registration is the \texttt{LiteralEntry} class template.
Its definition in the header reads as follows.

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Suffix, typename F> struct LiteralEntry
{
  F handler;
  static constexpr std::string_view suffix = Suffix.view ();

  explicit constexpr LiteralEntry (F f) : handler (std::move (f)) {}
};
\end{code}

The template has two parameters. The first, \texttt{Suffix}, is a
\texttt{FixedString} (see Chapter 4) used as a non-type template
argument; it carries the spelling of the literal at compile time. The
second, \texttt{F}, is the type of a callable that accepts a
\texttt{long double} and returns a value of arbitrary type. The handler
is stored by value as the \texttt{handler} member.

Each entry exposes its spelling as the static member \texttt{suffix},
which is a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
obtained from
\texttt{Suffix.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. This
member is what the \texttt{LiteralSet} consults at run time when
scanning the table. Because \texttt{suffix} is
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}}, it costs no per-instance storage
and the view can be taken without constructing any
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}.

The constructor is \texttt{explicit} and \texttt{constexpr}, so an entry
can be constructed in a constant expression. The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}move} on the parameter is benign
for typical lambdas but allows move-only callables to be registered
without copy. Note that the entry itself does no parsing; it merely
holds the spelling and the handler. Parsing is the responsibility of the
containing set.

The spelling carried by \texttt{Suffix} is interpreted as a suffix of
the input string by the set's \texttt{parse} function. This
suffix-oriented design is what makes the feature natural for
unit-decorated numerics such as
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km}, where the interesting
lexical structure is the trailing unit. For keyword tables the spelling
is compared against the whole token, which is simply the degenerate case
of a suffix match against a one-token input.

\section{The lit\textless\textgreater{} Factory}\label{ch17-the-lit-factory}

Constructing a \texttt{LiteralEntry} directly requires naming two
template arguments: the \texttt{FixedString} spelling and the deduced
handler type. The \texttt{lit} factory function deduces the handler type
so that the author only writes the spelling explicitly. Its definition
is:

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Suffix, typename F>
constexpr auto
lit (F &&f)
{
  return LiteralEntry<Suffix, std::decay_t<F>>{ std::forward<F> (f) };
}
\end{code}

The spelling is passed as an explicit template argument, writing the
literal text inside angle brackets as a string literal. The handler is
passed as an ordinary forwarding reference, and its decayed type is used
as the \texttt{F} argument to \texttt{LiteralEntry}. The result is a
prvalue of the entry type, suitable for passing to
\texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set}.

A typical use looks like the following, taken from the header's own
documentation.

\begin{code}
dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v){ return v * 1000.0L; })
\end{code}

The spelling \texttt{"\_\allowbreak{}km"} is a compile-time value. The
lambda is the handler; it receives the numeric prefix of the input and
returns the converted value. The return type of the handler is whatever
the lambda yields, here \texttt{long double}. Different entries in the
same set may have different handler return types, although the set's
\texttt{parse} function normalises the result to \texttt{long double} as
described below.

Because \texttt{Suffix} is a \texttt{FixedString} NTTP, the spelling
must be a compile-time constant. A spelling read from a configuration
file at run time cannot be passed to
\texttt{lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}; it
would have to be dispatched on manually. This is a deliberate
restriction that keeps the table static and the comparisons inlinable.

The factory is \texttt{constexpr}, so entries can be assembled during
constant evaluation. This is what allows a DSL to declare its
\texttt{literals} member as
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} auto literals =\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literal\_\allowbreak{}set(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
with the entire table built at compile time and placed in read-only
storage.

\section{The LiteralSet Container}\label{ch17-the-literalset-container}

A single entry is rarely useful in isolation. The \texttt{LiteralSet}
class template aggregates an arbitrary number of \texttt{LiteralEntry}
objects into a typed tuple and provides the \texttt{parse} member that
scans them. Its definition reads:

\begin{code}
template <typename... Entries> struct LiteralSet
{
  std::tuple<Entries...> entries;

  explicit constexpr LiteralSet (Entries... es) : entries (std::move (es)...)
  {
  }

  long double
  parse (std::string_view input) const
  {
    long double result = 0.0L;
    bool found = false;

    std::apply (
        [&] (const auto &...e)
          {
            (
                [&]
                  {
                    if (found)
                      return;
                    auto suf = e.suffix;
                    if (input.size () > suf.size ()
                        && input.substr (input.size () - suf.size ()) == suf)
                      {
                        auto num_str
                            = input.substr (0, input.size () - suf.size ());
                        long double num = std::stold (std::string (num_str));
                        result = e.handler (num);
                        found = true;
                      }
                  }(),
                ...);
          },
        entries);

    if (!found)
      {
        throw std::runtime_error (
            std::string ("dsl::LiteralSet::parse: no suffix matched for '")
            + std::string (input) + "'");
      }
    return result;
  }
};
\end{code}

The set is parameterised on the entry types, so the full type of the
table encodes the number and order of its rows. Two sets that register
the same spellings in a different order have different types. The
entries are stored in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}tuple\textless{}\allowbreak{}Entries.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
member named \texttt{entries}, constructed by perfect-forwarding each
argument into the tuple.

The constructor is \texttt{explicit} and \texttt{constexpr}. The
\texttt{explicit} qualifier prevents accidental implicit conversion from
a single entry to a one-element set, which would otherwise be a
surprising conversion path. The \texttt{constexpr} qualifier permits the
whole table to be assembled during constant evaluation.

The set is an aggregate of sorts: it has no virtual functions, no base
classes, and no invariants beyond the construction of its tuple. It is
therefore cheap to copy and cheap to store as a static member. The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}tuple} is the only non-trivial
member, and even it is composed entirely of trivially relocatable
callables in typical use.

\section{The literal\_set Variadic
Factory}\label{ch17-the-literal_set-variadic-factory}

As with \texttt{lit}, the toolkit provides a deducing factory so that
the author need not spell the entry types. The
\texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set} factory is:

\begin{code}
template <typename... Entries>
constexpr auto
literal_set (Entries &&...es)
{
  return LiteralSet<std::decay_t<Entries>...>{ std::forward<Entries> (es)... };
}
\end{code}

The factory forwards its arguments into a newly constructed
\texttt{LiteralSet} whose type arguments are the decayed types of the
arguments. Passing a mixture of \texttt{LiteralEntry} instantiations
produced by
\texttt{lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} yields a
\texttt{LiteralSet} whose \texttt{Entries} pack lists those exact entry
types. The result is a prvalue of the set type and may be bound to a
\texttt{constexpr auto} variable.

A typical table is therefore written without any explicit type names, as
in the following unit-conversion table drawn from the header examples.

\begin{code}
constexpr auto lits = dsl::literal_set(
    dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v){ return v * 1000.0L; }),
    dsl::lit<"_m"> ([](long double v){ return v; }),
    dsl::lit<"_cm">([](long double v){ return v / 100.0L; })
);
\end{code}

The variable \texttt{lits} has type
\texttt{LiteralSet\textless{}\allowbreak{}LiteralEntry\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}lambda1\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} LiteralEntry\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}lambda2\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} LiteralEntry\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}lambda3\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
where each lambda type is a distinct compiler-generated closure type.
Because the closure types are unique to the translation unit, the set
type is also unique and cannot be named in a header; the \texttt{auto}
placeholder is essential.

The factory is the only intended construction path for a
\texttt{LiteralSet}. Direct construction is possible but offers no
advantage, since the entry types are awkward to name. Once constructed,
the set is used either by calling its \texttt{parse} member directly or
by handing it to the \texttt{CustomLiterals} mixin as the
\texttt{Derived:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals} member, as
described below.

\section{The parse Member Function}\label{ch17-the-parse-member-function}

The \texttt{parse} member performs the actual classification. Its
contract is to treat the input string as a numeric prefix followed by a
suffix drawn from the table, to locate the first matching suffix, to
convert the prefix with \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}stold},
and to return the result of applying the matched handler to that number.
The return type is \texttt{long double} regardless of the handler's
return type; the handler's result is implicitly converted.

The matching condition is a suffix test. For an entry whose
\texttt{suffix} has length \texttt{n}, the entry matches when
\texttt{input.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} \textgreater{}\allowbreak{} n}
and the last \texttt{n} characters of \texttt{input} equal
\texttt{suffix}. The strict-greater comparison ensures that the numeric
prefix is non-empty; an input consisting only of the suffix, such as
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} with no leading digits, will not match an
entry whose suffix is \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km}.

When a match is found, the prefix is extracted as
\texttt{input.\allowbreak{}substr(\allowbreak{}0,\allowbreak{} input.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} - suf.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and passed to \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}stold}. The handler
is then invoked with the resulting \texttt{long double}, and its return
value is assigned to \texttt{result}. The \texttt{found} flag is set,
which short-circuits the remaining entries through the early-return at
the top of the fold-expression body.

The scan order is the declaration order of the entries in the set. The
first entry whose suffix matches the tail of the input wins; later
entries are not considered, even if they would also match. This
first-match semantics is the single most important behavioural detail of
the feature, and it has consequences for how overlapping spellings must
be ordered, as discussed below.

If no entry matches, \texttt{parse} throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error} with
a message of the form
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}LiteralSet:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse:\allowbreak{} no suffix match\allowbreak{}ed for '\textless{}\allowbreak{}input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}'}.
The function does not return a sentinel or an optional; the failure mode
is an exception. DSL authors who need softer failure handling must catch
the exception at the call site, or pre-scan the input themselves before
invoking \texttt{parse}.

\section{The CustomLiterals Feature Tag and
Mixin}\label{ch17-the-customliterals-feature-tag-and-mixin}

The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature tag integrates the literal table
with the CRTP foundation described in Chapter 3. By listing
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}CustomLiterals} among the feature
tags of a \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} specialisation,
the derived DSL acquires a \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} static
member that delegates to its \texttt{literals} member. The feature is
defined as:

\begin{code}
struct CustomLiterals
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    static long double
    parse_literal (std::string_view input)
      requires requires { Derived::literals; }
    {
      return Derived::literals.parse (input);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The tag is an empty struct whose only content is the nested
\texttt{Mixin} template. The mixin is injected into the CRTP base class
chain by the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} template, so
any DSL that lists \texttt{CustomLiterals} as a feature gains the
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} member. The member is static
because the literal table is itself static state of the derived class.

The
\texttt{requires require\allowbreak{}s \{ Derived:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals; \}}
clause is a constraint, not merely documentation. It prevents the mixin
from instantiating \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} for derived
classes that have not declared a \texttt{literals} member. Without that
member, the expression
\texttt{Derived:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals.\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}}
would be ill-formed, and the constraint makes the removal clean rather
than producing a cascade of template errors. The constraint is
re-expressed at the concept level by \texttt{HasLiterals}, described
next.

The derived class is responsible for declaring the \texttt{literals}
member with the correct type and value. The mixin does not synthesise
it; it merely references it. This separation keeps the feature tag
stateless and lets the derived class control the exact set of registered
spellings, including their order.

\section{The parse\_literal Entry
Point}\label{ch17-the-parse_literal-entry-point}

With the feature in place, parsing a literal string from the DSL becomes
a single static call. The derived class declares its table and inherits
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}, which forwards to the table's
\texttt{parse} member. The canonical form, taken from the header
documentation, is:

\begin{code}
struct UnitsDSL : dsl::DSL<UnitsDSL, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
    static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
        dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v){ return v * 1000.0L; }),
        dsl::lit<"_m"> ([](long double v){ return v; })
    );
};
auto val = UnitsDSL::parse_literal("5.0_km");  // 5000.0
\end{code}

The call
\texttt{UnitsDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}literal(\allowbreak{}"5.\allowbreak{}0\_\allowbreak{}km")\allowbreak{}}
resolves to the mixin's static member, which invokes
\texttt{UnitsDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals.\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}"5.\allowbreak{}0\_\allowbreak{}km")\allowbreak{}}.
The set scans its entries, finds that \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} matches
the tail, extracts \texttt{5.\allowbreak{}0}, converts it to
\texttt{long double}, applies the kilometre handler, and returns
\texttt{5000.\allowbreak{}0L}.

The return type is \texttt{long double} in all cases. A DSL whose
handlers return \texttt{int} or \texttt{double} will have those values
converted to \texttt{long double} before return. Callers that need the
original type must bypass \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} and call
\texttt{literals.\allowbreak{}parse} directly, bearing in mind that
\texttt{parse} also returns \texttt{long double}.

Because \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} is static, no instance of
the DSL is required. This reflects the fact that the literal table is
class-level configuration, not instance state. It also means that two
instances of the same DSL share the same table and produce the same
classification for the same input.

\section{The HasLiterals Concept}\label{ch17-the-hasliterals-concept}

The \texttt{HasLiterals} concept identifies types that participate in
the \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature correctly. It is defined alongside
the other feature concepts described in Chapter 5:

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
concept HasLiterals
    = HasFeature<T, CustomLiterals> && requires { T::literals; };
\end{code}

The concept has two clauses. The first,
\texttt{HasFeature\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} CustomLiterals\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
checks that \texttt{T} lists \texttt{CustomLiterals} among its feature
tags, which is the structural requirement that the mixin be present in
the base chain. The second,
\texttt{requires \{ T:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals; \}}, checks
that \texttt{T} actually declares a member named \texttt{literals} that
can be named in an unevaluated context.

Both clauses are necessary. A type could list the feature tag but forget
to declare \texttt{literals}, in which case the mixin's
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} would be constrained away and the
type would not actually support parsing. Conversely, a type could
declare a \texttt{literals} member without listing the feature tag, in
which case the mixin would not be present and
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} would not exist even though the
table does. \texttt{HasLiterals} requires both halves of the contract.

The concept is the recommended way to constrain templates that accept a
DSL with literal support. A parser combinator that wants to consult a
DSL's literal table, for example, can require
\texttt{HasLiterals\textless{}\allowbreak{}D\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
rather than re-implementing the two clauses inline. Generic code that
calls \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} should do so behind a
\texttt{requires HasLite\allowbreak{}rals\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
guard so that overload resolution removes the candidate cleanly for
non-literal DSLs.

\section{Matching Semantics: Suffix Match, First-Match
Ordering}\label{ch17-matching-semantics-suffix-match-first-match-ordering}

The behaviour of \texttt{parse} is best understood through its two
governing rules: the match is a suffix match, and the scan is a
first-match scan. A suffix match means that an entry matches the input
only when the input ends with the entry's spelling and the input is
strictly longer than the spelling. A first-match scan means that the
entries are tried in declaration order and the first match wins.

The suffix rule is what suits the feature to unit-decorated numerics.
The input \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km} ends with
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km}, so the \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} entry
matches and the prefix \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5} is parsed. The input
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5} alone does not end with any registered suffix,
so it does not match and \texttt{parse} throws. The input
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} alone, although it ends with
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km}, fails the strict-greater length test and
also does not match, because the numeric prefix would be empty and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}stold} would have nothing to
consume.

The first-match rule has consequences when one suffix is a tail of
another. Suppose a set registers \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m} before
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km}. The input
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km} ends with
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} but does not end with
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}, because the last two characters are
\texttt{km}, not \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}. The order is irrelevant in
that particular case. Now suppose a set registers \texttt{m} and
\texttt{5m}, and the input is \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5m}. The \texttt{m}
entry matches because the input ends with \texttt{m}. The \texttt{5m}
entry, declared later, is never reached, even though it also matches.
The handler for \texttt{m} wins.

This is the central ordering pitfall of the feature: when one suffix is
a proper tail of another and both can match the same input, the entry
declared first shadows the entry declared second. The implementation
does not perform longest-match selection. A user who needs \texttt{5m}
to win over \texttt{m} for the input \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5m} must
declare \texttt{5m} before \texttt{m} in the
\texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set} argument list.

The toolkit does not check for shadowing at compile time. Two entries
may have identical spellings, in which case the second is simply
unreachable. Two entries whose spellings overlap only in suffix-vs-tail
fashion are permitted, and the order of declaration silently determines
which one is consulted first. Disciplined tables keep overlapping
spellings ordered from most specific to least specific.

\section{Registering Keywords}\label{ch17-registering-keywords}

Although the feature is suffix-oriented, it adapts naturally to keyword
tables. A keyword is a token whose entire spelling matches a registered
literal. Because the suffix match degenerates to a whole-string match
when the prefix is required to be numeric, the keyword use case needs a
handler that ignores its numeric argument and yields a tag.

The trick is to provide a numeric prefix in the input. A lexer that has
already split the input into tokens can present each token together with
a leading numeric sentinel, or it can use a dedicated wrapper that calls
the handler with a dummy value. The more direct approach for keyword
classification is to bypass \texttt{parse} and iterate the entries
directly, since the suffix machinery is not the natural fit for
whole-token matching.

When the suffix machinery is acceptable, a keyword table can be built as
follows. The handler returns an enumeration tag identifying the keyword.

\begin{code}
enum class Kw { If, Then, Else, Unknown };

constexpr auto keywords = dsl::literal_set(
    dsl::lit<"if">([](long double){ return Kw::If; }),
    dsl::lit<"then">([](long double){ return Kw::Then; }),
    dsl::lit<"else">([](long double){ return Kw::Else; })
);
\end{code}

Because the suffix match requires a strictly longer input, a bare token
\texttt{if} will not match the entry \texttt{"if"}; the input must be
longer than the spelling. Keyword classification therefore typically
uses a small adapter that compares the whole token against each entry's
\texttt{suffix} directly, rather than calling \texttt{parse}. The
\texttt{LiteralSet} exposes its \texttt{entries} tuple publicly, so such
an adapter can iterate the entries with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}apply} and compare spellings
exactly.

This adaptation shows the boundary between the \texttt{CustomLiterals}
feature and a full lexer. For unit-suffixed numerics the feature is a
perfect fit; for whole-token keyword classification the feature provides
the table and the spelling storage, while the caller provides the
comparison strategy.

\section{Registering Operators}\label{ch17-registering-operators}

Operator tables raise the same considerations as keyword tables. An
operator such as \texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} is a short fixed
spelling, and a DSL that classifies operator tokens wants to map each
spelling to a tag. The suffix machinery of \texttt{parse} is not the
natural dispatch for an operator token, because the token contains no
numeric prefix, but the \texttt{LiteralEntry} record is still a
convenient way to pair a compile-time spelling with a tag.

A typical operator table stores the spelling and yields an operator tag
from the handler, ignoring the numeric argument exactly as the keyword
table does.

\begin{code}
enum class Op { Plus, Minus, Le, Ge };

constexpr auto operators = dsl::literal_set(
    dsl::lit<"+">([](long double){ return Op::Plus; }),
    dsl::lit<"-">([](long double){ return Op::Minus; }),
    dsl::lit<"<=">([](long double){ return Op::Le; }),
    dsl::lit<">=">([](long double){ return Op::Ge; })
);
\end{code}

Ordering matters for operators just as it does for unit suffixes. The
spelling \texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} is a proper tail of
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=}. If \texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}}
were registered and the dispatch were a suffix match, an input ending in
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} would not match
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} anyway because the last character is
\texttt{=}. For whole-token dispatch, however, a token
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} and a token
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} are distinct strings and there is no
shadowing. The lesson is that shadowing is a property of the matching
rule, not of the table alone.

For longest-match token classification across a set of operators and
keywords, the parser combinator framework of Chapter 23 is the
appropriate tool. The \texttt{CustomLiterals} table is best regarded as
a static spelling registry that the combinator layer can consult, rather
than as a complete lexer.

\section{A Unit-Suffix Numeric Literal
Set}\label{ch17-a-unit-suffix-numeric-literal-set}

The canonical application of the feature is a unit-conversion DSL. The
following DSL, drawn from the header examples, registers three length
units and exposes \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} through the
mixin.

\begin{code}
struct UnitsDSL : dsl::DSL<UnitsDSL, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
    static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
        dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v){ return v * 1000.0L; }),
        dsl::lit<"_m"> ([](long double v){ return v; }),
        dsl::lit<"_cm">([](long double v){ return v / 100.0L; })
    );
};
\end{code}

Calling
\texttt{UnitsDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}literal(\allowbreak{}"3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km")\allowbreak{}}
scans the table. The \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} entry matches the tail
of \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km}, the prefix
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5} is converted to \texttt{long double}, and the
handler multiplies by one thousand, returning
\texttt{3500.\allowbreak{}0L}. The \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m} and
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm} entries are not consulted because the first
match short-circuits the scan.

The same DSL handles \texttt{100\_\allowbreak{}cm} by matching
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm}, converting \texttt{100}, and dividing by one
hundred, yielding \texttt{1.\allowbreak{}0L}. An input such as
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5mi} matches no entry, and
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error} with
the message
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}LiteralSet:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse:\allowbreak{} no suffix match\allowbreak{}ed for '3.\allowbreak{}5mi'}.

Because the table is \texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}}, it is
constructed once and shared across all calls. There is no per-call
allocation, no hash-table lookup, and no virtual dispatch. The
fold-expression over the entry tuple is expanded by the compiler into a
straight-line sequence of comparisons, which optimisers typically reduce
further.

The ordering of the three entries above is safe because no suffix is a
tail of another: \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} ends with \texttt{m} but not
with \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}, \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm} ends with
\texttt{m} but not with \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}, and
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m} is distinct from both. A table that added a
bare \texttt{m} suffix would have to place \texttt{m} after
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} and \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm} to avoid
shadowing them on inputs such as
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km}, although as noted
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km} does not end with \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}
and so the bare \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m} would not in fact capture it;
the shadowing concern arises only for inputs that genuinely end with the
shorter spelling.

\section{A Tiny Token Classifier}\label{ch17-a-tiny-token-classifier}

To illustrate the feature as a building block inside a larger front end,
consider a small token classifier that uses a \texttt{LiteralSet} to map
operator spellings to tags. The classifier iterates the entries directly
rather than calling \texttt{parse}, because tokens carry no numeric
prefix. The \texttt{entries} member is public, so the iteration is
straightforward.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <string_view>
#include <tuple>
#include <iostream>

enum class Tok { Plus, Minus, Le, Ge, Unknown };

constexpr auto ops = dsl::literal_set(
    dsl::lit<"+">([](long double){ return Tok::Plus; }),
    dsl::lit<"-">([](long double){ return Tok::Minus; }),
    dsl::lit<"<=">([](long double){ return Tok::Le; }),
    dsl::lit<">=">([](long double){ return Tok::Ge; })
);

Tok classify(std::string_view tok) {
    Tok result = Tok::Unknown;
    std::apply(
        [&](const auto&... e) {
            (([&] {
                if (tok == e.suffix) result = e.handler(0.0L);
            }()), ...);
        },
        ops.entries);
    return result;
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{classify} function walks the entry tuple with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}apply} and a fold-expression,
comparing each token against the entry's \texttt{suffix} view. The first
exact match assigns the tag by invoking the handler with a dummy
\texttt{0.\allowbreak{}0L}. Because the comparison is exact, there is no
shadowing between \texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=}: a token
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} matches only the
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} entry, and a token
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} matches only the
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} entry.

This pattern shows how the \texttt{LiteralSet} doubles as a generic
spelling table. The \texttt{parse} member is the convenience path for
suffix-decorated numerics, but the underlying entry tuple is available
for any dispatch strategy the caller wishes to implement. The
compile-time spellings, the typed handlers, and the ordered tuple are
useful independently of the suffix convention.

For a production lexer, the parser combinators of Chapter 23 and the
primitive parsers of Chapter 24 offer a more complete solution,
including position tracking, diagnostics, and composition. The token
classifier above is intended only to show how \texttt{LiteralSet}
participates in a hand-written front end.

\section{Combining with the Pipeline
Feature}\label{ch17-combining-with-the-pipeline-feature}

Example 18 of the toolkit combines \texttt{CustomLiterals} with the
\texttt{Pipeline} feature described in Chapter 6. The DSL registers unit
suffixes through \texttt{CustomLiterals} and uses the \texttt{Pipeline}
mixin's \texttt{wrap} to feed the parsed value into a chain of
transformations. The example is reproduced below.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct MiniDSL : dsl::DSL<MiniDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
      dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }),
      dsl::lit<"_m">([](long double v) { return v; }));
};

int main() {
  auto meters = MiniDSL::parse_literal("2.5_km");
  auto shown = MiniDSL::wrap(meters) | dsl::pipe([](long double x) { return x + 10.0L; });
  std::cout << shown << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The two feature tags are listed together in the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} argument list. The CRTP base
class composes the two mixins, so \texttt{MiniDSL} gains both
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} from \texttt{CustomLiterals} and
\texttt{wrap} from \texttt{Pipeline}. The call
\texttt{MiniDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}literal(\allowbreak{}"2.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km")\allowbreak{}}
returns \texttt{2500.\allowbreak{}0L}, which is then wrapped and piped
through a lambda that adds ten, yielding \texttt{2510.\allowbreak{}0L}.

This composition is the intended use of the feature architecture. Each
feature contributes one capability, and the DSL author combines the
capabilities by listing the corresponding tags. The
\texttt{CustomLiterals} feature contributes the table and the
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} entry point; the \texttt{Pipeline}
feature contributes the \texttt{wrap} adapter and the \texttt{pipe}
operator; together they turn a string literal into a transformed numeric
value in a single expression.

The combination also illustrates the role of \texttt{long double} as the
lingua franca of the feature. The \texttt{Pipeline} in this example
carries \texttt{long double} values, and
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} returns \texttt{long double}, so
the two features interoperate without any explicit adapter. A DSL whose
handlers returned a different type would need a conversion at the
boundary, because \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} normalises to
\texttt{long double} regardless.

\section{Registering Constants}\label{ch17-registering-constants}

Beyond units and keywords, the feature can register named constants. A
constant is a literal whose handler ignores its numeric argument and
returns a fixed value. The spelling serves as the name, and the table
maps the name to its value. Because \texttt{parse} requires a numeric
prefix, the constant use case again benefits from direct entry iteration
rather than the \texttt{parse} member.

A constants table can be built with
\texttt{lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} using
handlers that capture the constant value by copy.

\begin{code}
constexpr auto constants = dsl::literal_set(
    dsl::lit<"pi">([](long double){ return 3.141592653589793238462643L; }),
    dsl::lit<"e">([](long double){ return 2.718281828459045235360287L; }),
    dsl::lit<"tau">([](long double){ return 6.283185307179586476925286L; })
);
\end{code}

A caller that iterates \texttt{constants.\allowbreak{}entries} and
compares spellings exactly can resolve the name \texttt{pi} to its
value. The handler's \texttt{long double} parameter is unused and is
present only to satisfy the
\texttt{F(\allowbreak{}long double)\allowbreak{} -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} T}
signature expected by \texttt{LiteralEntry}. This is a minor awkwardness
of the unified handler shape, which is tuned for the numeric-suffix
case.

For numeric-suffixed constants, such as a hypothetical \texttt{1k}
meaning one thousand, the \texttt{parse} member is again the natural
path: the input \texttt{1k} ends with \texttt{k}, the prefix \texttt{1}
is converted, and the handler multiplies by one thousand. The same table
can therefore mix named constants and scaled numerics, provided the
spellings are chosen so that the suffix matches disambiguate correctly.

\section{Design Notes: Why the Suffix
Convention}\label{ch17-design-notes-why-the-suffix-convention}

The suffix convention may seem restrictive for a feature named ``custom
literals'' in general. The restriction is a deliberate alignment with
the dominant use case: unit-decorated numerics, where the lexical
structure is a number followed by a unit marker. By fixing the matching
rule to a suffix test, the feature can offer a single \texttt{parse}
member that handles this case without any configuration, and the handler
signature can be fixed to
\texttt{F(\allowbreak{}long double)\allowbreak{} -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} T}
so that the table type is uniform.

The cost of this design is that whole-token classification, as for
keywords and operators, is not directly served by \texttt{parse}. The
toolkit addresses that cost by exposing the \texttt{entries} tuple, so a
caller can implement any matching rule against the same compile-time
spellings. The \texttt{LiteralSet} is thus both a parser for the
numeric-suffix case and a generic spelling table for other cases.

The first-match ordering is a similar trade-off. A longest-match rule
would remove the shadowing pitfall but would require the implementation
to scan all entries and select the longest match, which is more work per
call and which complicates the handler dispatch. First-match is simpler,
is predictable, and lets the author express an explicit priority by
ordering the entries. The author who wants longest-match can achieve it
by ordering entries from longest spelling to shortest, so that the first
match is also the longest.

\section{The Lifecycle of a Literal
Set}\label{ch17-the-lifecycle-of-a-literal-set}

A \texttt{LiteralSet} is a value type with no ownership beyond its tuple
of callables. Lambdas with empty captures are stateless and occupy no
storage, so a set composed of captureless lambdas is effectively empty
at run time, even though its type encodes a rich table. Sets that
capture state, such as a lookup table captured by copy, do carry that
state in the tuple.

The intended lifetime of a set is static. A
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}} member of a DSL class is
constructed during constant initialisation and lives for the duration of
the program. A set can also be a local \texttt{constexpr} variable, in
which case it lives for the enclosing scope. Passing a set by const
reference to a function is cheap, because the type is small and the
spellings are static.

Sets are not mutated after construction. The \texttt{parse} member is
\texttt{const}, and there is no public interface for adding or removing
entries. A DSL that needs a different table at run time must select
between pre-built sets, rather than mutating one set. This immutability
is consistent with the compile-time nature of the spellings: since the
spellings are template arguments, the type of the set cannot change at
run time, and so the set itself is treated as immutable.

The exception thrown on failure is the only observable side effect of
\texttt{parse}. The function performs no allocation, no I/O, and no
global-state mutation. The \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}stold}
call may throw
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}invalid\_\allowbreak{}argument}
or
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}out\_\allowbreak{}of\_\allowbreak{}range}
if the numeric prefix is malformed, even when the suffix matches; this
exception propagates through \texttt{parse} to the caller. A robust
caller should be prepared to catch either the runtime error from a
missing suffix or the conversion exceptions from a malformed prefix.

\section{A Complete Units Example}\label{ch17-a-complete-units-example}

The following complete program defines a units DSL, parses several
suffixed numerics, and prints the results. It is a superset of the
header example and is written to compile with \texttt{-std=c++20}.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct UnitsDSL : dsl::DSL<UnitsDSL, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
    static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
        dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v){ return v * 1000.0L; }),
        dsl::lit<"_m"> ([](long double v){ return v; }),
        dsl::lit<"_cm">([](long double v){ return v / 100.0L; }),
        dsl::lit<"_mm">([](long double v){ return v / 1000.0L; })
    );
};

int main() {
    std::cout << UnitsDSL::parse_literal("3.5_km") << "\n";  // 3500
    std::cout << UnitsDSL::parse_literal("100_cm") << "\n";  // 1
    std::cout << UnitsDSL::parse_literal("750_mm") << "\n";  // 0.75
    try {
        UnitsDSL::parse_literal("3.5mi");
    } catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
}
\end{code}

The four entries are ordered safely: \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}km},
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}m}, \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}cm},
\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}mm} are pairwise distinct as suffixes, and none
is a tail of another that would cause shadowing on the test inputs. The
output is \texttt{3500}, \texttt{1}, \texttt{0.\allowbreak{}75}, and the
diagnostic message for the unmatched \texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5mi} input.

The program also demonstrates the failure path. The input
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5mi} matches none of the four suffixes, so
\texttt{parse} throws and the \texttt{catch} block reports the message.
A DSL that wanted to treat unmatched input as zero, rather than as an
error, would wrap the call in a helper that catches the exception and
returns a default.

\section{The HasLiterals Concept in
Use}\label{ch17-the-hasliterals-concept-in-use}

Generic code that accepts a DSL with literal support should constrain
its template parameter with \texttt{HasLiterals}. The following function
template parses a value from any literal-supporting DSL and applies a
scaling factor, returning zero on failure.

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
requires dsl::HasLiterals<D>
long double scaled_value(std::string_view s, long double factor) {
    try {
        return D::parse_literal(s) * factor;
    } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
        return 0.0L;
    }
}
\end{code}

The constraint ensures that
\texttt{D:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} exists
and is callable. Without the constraint, the call would be ill-formed
for a DSL that lacks the \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature, and the error
would surface deep inside the template instantiation. With the
constraint, overload resolution removes the candidate for non-literal
DSLs, and the diagnostic is local to the call site.

The concept also serves as documentation. A reader who sees
\texttt{requires dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}HasLiterals\textless{}\allowbreak{}D\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
knows immediately that the function depends on the literal table,
without inspecting the body. This is the same role that the other
feature concepts of Chapter 5 play for their respective features.

A function that needs to combine literal support with another feature
can conjoin the concepts. For example, a function that requires both
literal parsing and pipeline wrapping would write
\texttt{requires dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}HasLiterals\textless{}\allowbreak{}D\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} \&\& dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}HasPipeline\textless{}\allowbreak{}D\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
assuming a corresponding \texttt{HasPipeline} concept. The feature
concepts are designed to compose in this way, reflecting the
orthogonality of the features themselves.

\section{Pitfalls and Common
Mistakes}\label{ch17-pitfalls-and-common-mistakes}

The first pitfall is misspelling the suffix. Because the spelling is a
\texttt{FixedString} non-type template argument, a typo such as
\texttt{"\_\allowbreak{}Kn"} instead of \texttt{"\_\allowbreak{}km"}
compiles cleanly but never matches the intended inputs. The table
silently fails to recognise the unit, and \texttt{parse} throws at run
time. A table should be tested against representative inputs to catch
such typos early.

The second pitfall is the strict-greater length test. An input that
consists of the suffix alone, with no numeric prefix, does not match.
Calling
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal(\allowbreak{}"\_\allowbreak{}km")\allowbreak{}}
throws, because the input length equals the suffix length and the
strict-greater test fails. Callers that present whole tokens to
\texttt{parse} must ensure that the token carries a numeric prefix, or
they must use the direct-entry-iteration pattern shown earlier.

The third pitfall is shadowing between overlapping suffixes. When one
suffix is a proper tail of another and both can match the same input,
the entry declared first wins. The implementation does not perform
longest-match selection. Tables that mix overlapping spellings must
order them from most specific to least specific to avoid unintended
shadowing. The ordering is a property of the table that the author must
maintain.

The fourth pitfall is handler return-type conversion. The \texttt{parse}
member returns \texttt{long double} regardless of the handler's return
type. A handler that returns \texttt{int} will have its result widened,
with possible loss of precision for very large values. A handler that
returns a non-numeric type will fail to compile, because the assignment
to \texttt{result} requires a conversion to \texttt{long double}.
Handlers should be written with this conversion in mind.

The fifth pitfall is the exception on no match. Code that calls
\texttt{parse} or \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} must be prepared
to catch
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error}, or
it must pre-validate the input. A loop that parses many inputs and lets
the exception propagate will abort on the first unrecognised input,
which is rarely the desired behaviour for a batch lexer.

The sixth pitfall is assuming that the feature is a general lexer. It is
not. The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature is a static, suffix-oriented
spelling table with a first-match dispatch. For full lexer concerns such
as position tracking, character classes, longest-match across
alternatives, and diagnostics, the parser combinator framework of
Chapter 23 and the PEG machinery of Chapter 27 are the appropriate
tools. The literal table can feed into those frameworks, but it does not
replace them.

\section{Interoperability with Native User-Defined
Literals}\label{ch17-interoperability-with-native-user-defined-literals}

The header documentation notes that the feature does not itself define
native C++ user-defined literal operators such as
\texttt{operator""\_\allowbreak{}km}. Native UDL syntax, writing
\texttt{3.\allowbreak{}5\_\allowbreak{}km} directly in C++ source,
requires an \texttt{operator""\_\allowbreak{}km} defined in an inline
namespace, which is a language feature separate from the
\texttt{CustomLiterals} table. The header suggests defining such
operators in the user's own header when native syntax is desired.

The two mechanisms are complementary. The \texttt{CustomLiterals} table
parses strings at run time, while native UDL operators parse literals at
compile time in C++ source. A DSL that wants both can define its table
with \texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set} for run-time string parsing and
can additionally provide \texttt{operator""} overloads that invoke the
same conversion logic for compile-time use. The following sketch shows
the pattern.

\begin{code}
inline namespace unit_literals {
    constexpr long double operator""_km(long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }
    constexpr long double operator""_m (long double v) { return v; }
}
// Usage:
// using namespace unit_literals;
// auto d = 3.5_km;  // 3500.0
\end{code}

The native operators are entirely the user's responsibility. The toolkit
provides the table and the \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} entry
point; it does not generate the operators. This separation keeps the
feature focused on run-time string classification and leaves the
compile-time UDL machinery, which has its own language rules, to the
user.

\section{Summary}\label{ch17-summary}

The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature provides a compile-time registry of
named string spellings and a run-time classifier that consults it. The
registry is built from \texttt{LiteralEntry} records, each pairing a
\texttt{FixedString} spelling with a handler callable. The
\texttt{lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} factory
deduces the handler type so that only the spelling must be written
explicitly. The \texttt{literal\_\allowbreak{}set} variadic factory
aggregates entries into a \texttt{LiteralSet}, which stores them as a
typed tuple and exposes a \texttt{parse} member.

The \texttt{parse} member treats its input as a numeric prefix followed
by a suffix drawn from the table. It scans the entries in declaration
order and applies the handler of the first entry whose suffix matches
the tail of the input. The first-match rule means that overlapping
suffixes must be ordered carefully: the entry declared first shadows
later entries that would also match. There is no longest-match
selection; authors who need longest-match must enforce it by ordering
entries from most specific to least specific. On no match,
\texttt{parse} throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error}.

The \texttt{CustomLiterals} feature tag integrates the table with the
CRTP foundation. A DSL that lists the tag and declares a
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} auto literals} member gains a
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} static entry point that delegates
to the table. The \texttt{HasLiterals} concept constrains types that
satisfy this contract and is the recommended way to guard generic code
that calls \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}.

The feature composes orthogonally with other features. Combined with the
\texttt{Pipeline} feature of Chapter 6, it turns a string literal into a
transformed numeric value in a single expression. Combined with the
parser combinators of Chapter 23 and the primitive parsers of Chapter
24, it provides a static spelling table that a larger front end can
consult. For whole-token classification, such as keyword and operator
tables, the \texttt{entries} tuple is available for direct iteration,
since the \texttt{parse} member's suffix convention is tuned for
numeric-decorated inputs rather than for bare tokens.

The feature is narrow by design. It is not a general lexer, it does not
perform longest-match selection, and it does not generate native C++ UDL
operators. Within its scope, it offers a typed, ordered, compile-time
spelling table with a predictable first-match dispatch, suitable for
unit-suffixed numerics and as a building block for larger front ends.
